WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.660
I want you to look at your screen for a second, or I mean just think about how you navigate the internet on any given Tuesday.

00:07.460 --> 00:09.560
It is pretty chaotic, if we're being honest.

00:09.920 --> 00:13.960
Oh, totally. Just think about the absolute chaotic trail you leave behind.

00:14.600 --> 00:18.920
You probably have, like, what, 20 tabs open right now across three different windows?

00:19.300 --> 00:20.720
At least 20, yeah.

00:21.040 --> 00:27.160
Right. You're bookmarking articles you swear you'll read later, you're dropping random links into group chats,

00:27.160 --> 00:32.440
maybe starting a repository on GitHub or just liking a post just to find it again?

00:33.220 --> 00:35.120
And from the inside, it feels completely random.

00:35.420 --> 00:38.280
Exactly. It feels like you're just wandering.

00:39.320 --> 00:47.440
But what if those individual, highly personal breadcrumbs you're dropping were actually, like, building a permanent, invisible architecture?

00:48.060 --> 00:51.300
Yeah, an architecture that literally dictates human progress.

00:51.520 --> 00:56.540
Right. And determines what the very next person who logs on is capable of discovering.

00:56.540 --> 00:59.340
I mean, it forces a profound shift in perspective.

00:59.600 --> 01:04.760
We are so conditioned to think of our digital wandering as just, well, passive consumption.

01:05.220 --> 01:07.420
Yeah, we just browse, read, scroll.

01:07.680 --> 01:08.160
Exactly.

01:08.740 --> 01:12.920
We rarely consider the possibility that by simply walking through the digital forest,

01:13.040 --> 01:16.180
we are actively constructing the terrain for everyone else.

01:16.340 --> 01:19.460
And that brings us to the sole source material for our deep dive today.

01:19.460 --> 01:27.560
We are looking at this brilliant, somewhat dense, 2026 paper by an independent researcher named Flyxion.

01:28.000 --> 01:28.320
Yes.

01:28.640 --> 01:32.680
Titled, Return Paths and Residual Deposits in Distributed Exploration.

01:32.960 --> 01:33.220
Right.

01:33.900 --> 01:36.740
And our mission today is to figure out this massive idea.

01:37.400 --> 01:45.520
How the way you navigate information and the invisible residue you leave behind fundamentally alters the landscape for whoever comes next.

01:45.520 --> 01:50.680
And the wildest part of this whole premise is that it happens without a central architect.

01:51.100 --> 01:52.140
Nobody is in charge.

01:52.960 --> 01:54.360
Okay, let's unpack this.

01:54.900 --> 01:57.760
Before we can understand how we build this massive environment,

01:58.020 --> 02:02.080
we first need to look at the mechanics of how we actually move through it as individuals.

02:02.600 --> 02:05.000
That is definitely the necessary starting point.

02:05.760 --> 02:09.540
Flyxion begins by examining what they call recursive exploration.

02:09.820 --> 02:10.920
Okay, what does that look like?

02:10.920 --> 02:14.440
Well, imagine you are dropped into a massive directed graph.

02:14.640 --> 02:19.700
Think of like a social media platform or a vast network of code repositories.

02:19.960 --> 02:20.740
Okay, I'm with you.

02:20.880 --> 02:26.900
You find a fascinating node, maybe some obscure researcher, and you want to see who they're connected to.

02:27.520 --> 02:30.740
Now, they follow 10 people, but they have 10,000 followers.

02:31.320 --> 02:34.340
Which list do you check to find your next connection?

02:35.040 --> 02:37.240
I mean, logically, I'm going to look at the list of 10.

02:37.460 --> 02:40.620
Going through 10,000 followers sounds like an absolute nightmare.

02:40.920 --> 02:42.660
Which is human nature, right?

02:42.860 --> 02:43.060
Right.

02:43.220 --> 02:46.720
And it's what the paper formally calls the smaller neighborhood rule.

02:46.900 --> 02:49.920
You inherently inspect the smaller of the two bounds.

02:50.060 --> 02:50.520
Makes sense.

02:50.760 --> 02:54.260
But Flaxshin makes a very counterintuitive distinction here.

02:54.900 --> 02:58.820
Inspecting that smaller neighborhood doesn't actually increase your yield.

02:59.020 --> 02:59.520
Wait, really?

02:59.880 --> 03:03.960
Yeah, it doesn't mean the people in that list of 10 are inherently more valuable

03:03.960 --> 03:06.120
or more likely to be what you're looking for.

03:06.420 --> 03:07.440
So why do we do it?

03:07.760 --> 03:09.680
I mean, if it doesn't give us better results.

03:09.680 --> 03:13.600
Because it mathematically reduces the branching factor of your search.

03:14.120 --> 03:18.460
If you start with the 10,000 followers, your next step has 10,000 branches.

03:19.060 --> 03:19.960
Oh, wow, yeah.

03:20.220 --> 03:21.300
You'd just be paralyzed.

03:21.600 --> 03:21.900
Exactly.

03:22.220 --> 03:27.080
By choosing the list of 10, you are simply bounding the effort required to take the next step.

03:27.400 --> 03:31.160
It's about cognitive load, not necessarily the quality of the outcome.

03:31.620 --> 03:32.840
Okay, that makes a lot of sense.

03:32.840 --> 03:34.240
But here is the catch.

03:34.800 --> 03:40.180
As you go down this rabbit hole, clicking from the list of 10 to another list of 5 to another list of 20,

03:40.360 --> 03:44.620
you know, descending level after level, you eventually need a way to keep your bearings.

03:45.200 --> 03:48.520
Right, because if you don't, you're just totally lost in the network.

03:48.880 --> 03:49.340
Precisely.

03:49.340 --> 03:54.060
And this necessity is where Fibration introduces the concept of the vine.

03:54.560 --> 03:55.960
Okay, the vine.

03:56.560 --> 03:57.360
I like that imagery.

03:57.820 --> 04:05.760
Like a jungle explorer tying a rope to a tree at the edge of the forest before walking into the thicket just so they can always find their way back.

04:06.000 --> 04:07.980
That captures the utility of it perfectly.

04:08.360 --> 04:11.100
A vine is a return structure in your daily life.

04:11.200 --> 04:12.880
It's, you know, your browser history.

04:12.960 --> 04:13.560
Or the back button.

04:13.560 --> 04:18.620
The back button, a return stack, and software programming, or even just leaving a tab open for three weeks.

04:19.040 --> 04:19.720
Guilty as charged.

04:19.900 --> 04:20.080
Right.

04:20.580 --> 04:24.440
It is a mechanism that allows the explorer to maintain their point of departure.

04:25.100 --> 04:31.220
Enphylaxion actually grounds this by referencing Harry Harlow's famous 1958 cloth surrogate monkey experiments.

04:31.480 --> 04:32.800
Oh, right, the psychology study.

04:32.920 --> 04:33.180
Yes.

04:33.900 --> 04:39.020
Harlow showed that infant monkeys given a soft cloth mother used it as a secure base.

04:39.360 --> 04:43.220
Having that secure base made them far more willing to explore a strange room.

04:43.560 --> 04:43.980
Interesting.

04:44.500 --> 04:51.060
However, the paper is very strict in clarifying that in digital exploration, this secure base is entirely functional.

04:51.340 --> 04:52.700
So it's not about feeling safe.

04:52.900 --> 04:53.120
Right.

04:53.260 --> 04:55.220
It has nothing to do with psychological comfort.

04:55.560 --> 04:59.120
It mathematically lowers the expected cost of an exploratory commitment.

04:59.460 --> 05:07.240
You know, I actually have to push back a little on the monkey analogy because to me, reading this, maternal comfort just feels like the wrong framework entirely.

05:07.520 --> 05:08.440
Oh, how so?

05:08.840 --> 05:12.000
Isn't this just like a save point in a video game?

05:12.000 --> 05:13.920
Well, let's follow that logic.

05:14.540 --> 05:16.700
How does a save point change your behavior?

05:17.380 --> 05:17.940
Well, think about it.

05:17.960 --> 05:26.860
If you're playing a game and you know you just saved your progress at a glowing crystal, you're totally willing to walk into the dark, terrifying cave to see what's inside.

05:27.160 --> 05:28.320
Because the risk is gone.

05:28.620 --> 05:28.860
Right.

05:29.080 --> 05:29.920
You'll fight the boss.

05:30.380 --> 05:31.260
You'll jump the chasm.

05:31.260 --> 05:34.640
The structure itself encourages risk-taking.

05:35.080 --> 05:40.740
But if you knew that dying in the cave meant losing, like, 40 hours of game progress, you'd never go in.

05:41.300 --> 05:42.800
You'd just stay in the village forever.

05:43.540 --> 05:44.820
That is a really great analogy.

05:45.500 --> 05:46.700
The vine doesn't comfort you.

05:46.700 --> 05:49.140
It just lowers the cost of taking a risk.

05:49.920 --> 05:53.660
That is actually a far more accurate mechanical translation of Flyxion's point.

05:54.340 --> 06:03.380
The availability of the return structure, the vine, it doesn't force you into the cave, but it radically alters the cost-benefit analysis of wandering into the unknown.

06:04.000 --> 06:06.180
Yeah, because you aren't afraid of losing your place.

06:06.300 --> 06:07.220
You explore further.

06:07.480 --> 06:08.440
You dig deeper.

06:08.780 --> 06:10.900
You interact with more obscure nodes.

06:10.900 --> 06:14.340
But, as you noted, the save point is entirely private.

06:14.480 --> 06:15.400
It only helps you.

06:17.000 --> 06:26.760
The real thesis of this paper is what happens when thousands of people are navigating with their own private vines, and we start looking at the trail they leave in their wake.

06:27.180 --> 06:27.540
Right.

06:27.880 --> 06:38.320
Because while I have my private save point, as I'm wandering through that dark cave, I might be dropping glow sticks to light the way, or, I don't know, hacking away at some virtual vines to make a path.

06:38.460 --> 06:38.860
Exactly.

06:38.860 --> 06:42.160
And that brings us to the public infrastructure we leave behind.

06:42.820 --> 06:46.180
The paper calls this collective public residue the berm.

06:46.960 --> 06:48.140
Yes, the berm.

06:48.320 --> 06:51.680
This is where individual action becomes collective architecture.

06:52.360 --> 06:57.800
The paper defines the berm as an accumulation of residue that permanently changes the conditions of later movement.

06:58.240 --> 06:59.920
But without anyone planning it, right?

07:00.280 --> 07:01.140
Crucially, yes.

07:01.740 --> 07:06.360
This happens without the residue ever being deliberately designed as a road or a wall.

07:06.360 --> 07:12.580
To explain how this is possible, Flyxion leans heavily on a biological concept called stigmergy.

07:13.420 --> 07:13.900
Stigmergy.

07:14.600 --> 07:15.800
I mean, I'm going to be honest.

07:15.860 --> 07:19.560
That sounds like a chronic condition you get from reading too many academic PDFs.

07:19.560 --> 07:22.140
Yeah, it really does have that clinical ring to it.

07:22.240 --> 07:26.880
But it was actually coined in 1959 by a French biologist named Pierre-Paul Grassi.

07:27.300 --> 07:27.560
Okay.

07:27.620 --> 07:28.340
What was he studying?

07:28.760 --> 07:30.680
He spent his life studying termites.

07:31.180 --> 07:32.740
He was trying to solve this massive puzzle.

07:32.740 --> 07:40.000
How do thousands of blind insects build 20-foot-tall, structurally sound, climate-controlled towers without an architect?

07:40.440 --> 07:43.100
Right, because termites don't have, like, town hall meetings.

07:43.600 --> 07:45.780
No, they don't have foremen holding blueprints.

07:46.380 --> 07:50.740
So Grassi realized they coordinate through persistent modification of the environment.

07:51.040 --> 07:51.640
How does that work?

07:51.940 --> 07:56.240
Well, one termite drops a tiny pellet of mud that is infused with a specific pheromone.

07:56.480 --> 07:58.480
Okay, so the mud isn't just a building block.

07:58.560 --> 07:59.560
It's like a message.

08:00.020 --> 08:00.500
Precisely.

08:00.720 --> 08:03.760
But it's a message that doesn't require direct communication.

08:04.420 --> 08:11.540
When another termite happens to wander by and encounters that specific pheromone, it triggers an instinct to drop its own pellet in the exact same spot.

08:11.780 --> 08:12.200
Oh, wow.

08:12.200 --> 08:18.440
Over time, these individual, completely uncoordinated deposits stack up to build a massive archway.

08:19.140 --> 08:21.900
Stigma-G is coordination through the environment itself.

08:22.340 --> 08:25.320
So in our digital world, we are essentially the termites.

08:25.880 --> 08:26.240
We are.

08:26.240 --> 08:37.280
And our deposits are, you know, pheromone-laced mud pellets are the code we push to GitHub, the interfaces we design, the Wikipedia edits we make, or even just the links we share on a forum.

08:37.680 --> 08:39.780
That is the core mapping Fraxion is making.

08:40.100 --> 08:45.700
But the paper takes it a step further by breaking this human residue down into two distinct categories.

08:45.920 --> 08:46.540
Okay, what are they?

08:47.180 --> 08:49.180
The first is executable residue.

08:49.600 --> 08:53.900
Think of a working script, a piece of compiled code, or even a functional workaround you post on Reddit.

08:54.100 --> 08:54.680
Okay, got it.

08:54.680 --> 08:58.300
The paper notes that this acts on the intensive margin.

08:58.880 --> 09:00.860
The second kind is declarative residue.

09:01.420 --> 09:05.160
This is a specification document, an API interface contract, a blueprint.

09:05.560 --> 09:07.620
This acts on the extensive margin.

09:08.140 --> 09:08.360
Hold on.

09:08.440 --> 09:14.440
Let's ground those terms because intensive and extensive margins can sound a bit like economic word salad.

09:14.620 --> 09:14.980
Fair enough.

09:15.320 --> 09:19.940
Are we basically saying one makes things faster and the other changes what's actually possible?

09:19.940 --> 09:23.020
Here's where it gets really interesting.

09:23.400 --> 09:31.380
If we think about this in a physical workspace, executable residue, the intensive margin, is like leaving a power drill behind on a workbench.

09:31.700 --> 09:32.700
Okay, I like this.

09:32.700 --> 09:38.120
If I walk into the shop to build a table and find your drill, it cheapens the cost of my labor.

09:38.220 --> 09:38.940
It makes me faster.

09:39.660 --> 09:43.900
I could have built the table with a handsaw, but the drill makes my work intensive and efficient.

09:44.460 --> 09:45.480
Let's build on that.

09:45.820 --> 09:50.520
If the drill is the executable residue, what is the declarative residue in your workshop?

09:50.520 --> 09:57.240
Um, the declarative residue would be if you left the actual blueprints for the table sitting on the desk.

09:57.340 --> 09:57.580
Yes.

09:58.020 --> 10:07.700
That acts on the extensive margin because it completely removes the hardest cognitive part of the process, deciding what kind of table to build, how many legs it needs, what the dimensions are.

10:07.780 --> 10:08.180
Exactly.

10:08.500 --> 10:10.060
It enables me to start building immediately.

10:10.060 --> 10:16.100
But, and this is the crazy part, it also silently forces me to build your version of the table.

10:16.260 --> 10:16.520
Right.

10:16.880 --> 10:20.560
If I use your blueprint, I don't even realize my choices have been foreclosed.

10:20.640 --> 10:23.060
I'm just blindly following your specs because they were there.

10:23.460 --> 10:26.560
That distinction is the beating heart of Flyxion's argument.

10:27.180 --> 10:32.640
The blueprint transfers the previous builder's design decisions directly into the new builder's inherited conditions.

10:32.940 --> 10:35.260
Yeah, you didn't decide to make the table 30 inches high.

10:35.700 --> 10:38.280
The residue you inherited made that decision for you.

10:38.280 --> 10:41.440
You just accepted it because it was easier than drawing a new blueprint.

10:42.220 --> 10:45.780
And that insight brings us to the most dangerous precipice in the paper.

10:46.380 --> 10:47.680
The dark side of the berm.

10:48.240 --> 10:48.560
Yes.

10:48.940 --> 10:53.180
We have this pervasive cultural bias that accumulation is inherently good.

10:53.560 --> 10:57.180
We assume that standing on the shoulders of giants means we are always reaching higher.

10:57.660 --> 11:01.080
But Flyocron asks, what if the giant is stumbling?

11:01.500 --> 11:01.860
Right.

11:02.200 --> 11:07.880
Because if a blueprint forces a specific design, and that design is fundamentally flawed,

11:08.280 --> 11:10.300
we aren't just building one broken table.

11:10.660 --> 11:11.100
No, we're not.

11:11.220 --> 11:15.820
We are forcing the next thousand people who walk into the workshop to build broken tables.

11:16.440 --> 11:23.080
What's fascinating here is that Flyocron explicitly warns us about the concepts of path dependence and lock-in,

11:23.680 --> 11:27.780
citing classic economic models from Brian Arthur and Paul David back in the 1980s.

11:27.780 --> 11:30.620
Oh, right, where early choices get stuck.

11:31.000 --> 11:31.520
Exactly.

11:32.140 --> 11:37.400
The idea is that early, sometimes completely arbitrary choices become locked into a system

11:37.400 --> 11:42.280
simply because later actors find it vastly cheaper to conform to the established standard

11:42.280 --> 11:44.340
than to depart from it and start over.

11:44.720 --> 11:47.280
So the berm is not intrinsically beneficial.

11:47.680 --> 11:48.180
Not at all.

11:48.460 --> 11:50.200
If we aren't careful, the berm is a trap.

11:50.200 --> 11:54.180
So how does a bad idea actually get locked into the architecture?

11:55.060 --> 11:58.900
Because surely someone eventually notices the tables wobbly, right?

11:59.800 --> 12:04.120
Does the paper break down the mechanics of how a bad idea sneaks past our defenses?

12:04.660 --> 12:05.480
It does.

12:05.580 --> 12:08.900
And it requires understanding the strict failure chain of stigmergy.

12:09.680 --> 12:09.960
Okay.

12:10.220 --> 12:10.660
Lay it on me.

12:11.320 --> 12:14.380
For any deposit to become part of the berm, three things must happen.

12:14.700 --> 12:16.540
First, it must be encountered.

12:16.540 --> 12:20.320
I mean, if you write brilliant code, but no one finds it, it's just a dead trace.

12:20.780 --> 12:21.080
Makes sense.

12:21.720 --> 12:23.420
Second, it must be interpreted.

12:24.160 --> 12:29.000
Flyxion borrows a concept from researcher John Anderson here called corpus congruence.

12:29.480 --> 12:30.880
Corpus congruence.

12:31.360 --> 12:32.040
Okay, let me guess.

12:32.300 --> 12:38.860
I can only interpret your code if my background knowledge, my personal corpus, aligns somewhat with yours.

12:39.520 --> 12:40.200
Spot on.

12:40.580 --> 12:44.680
Like, if a quantum physics paper drops in front of me, my corpus congruence is zero.

12:44.680 --> 12:46.220
I literally can't read it.

12:46.540 --> 12:47.020
Exactly.

12:47.740 --> 12:51.160
If the deposit is too alien, you ignore it because you can't parse it.

12:51.460 --> 12:54.460
If it's too familiar, you ignore it because you already know it.

12:54.920 --> 12:55.840
So it has to be just right.

12:56.320 --> 12:56.580
Yes.

12:56.700 --> 13:02.080
It has to sit in that Goldilocks zone of being just adjacent enough to your current knowledge for you to understand it.

13:02.560 --> 13:06.820
And finally, if it is encountered and interpreted, it must be taken.

13:07.320 --> 13:07.720
Uptake.

13:08.080 --> 13:09.900
You have to actually implement it in your own work.

13:10.040 --> 13:10.340
Right.

13:10.340 --> 13:18.560
But this three-step chain is where Flyxion identifies a massive vulnerability, the unmarked provisional specification.

13:18.980 --> 13:21.100
The unmarked provisional specification.

13:21.100 --> 13:24.180
I mean, that sounds like academic alphabet soup.

13:24.300 --> 13:26.420
What is that actually seen in plain English?

13:26.620 --> 13:29.600
I mean, is it essentially a wild guess that someone forgot to put a warning label on?

13:30.140 --> 13:30.640
Oh, no.

13:31.160 --> 13:35.660
Let's walk through the formal model the paper uses, which is example 7.12.

13:35.660 --> 13:39.480
It lays out the mathematics of how a defective idea spreads.

13:39.860 --> 13:39.880
Okay.

13:40.260 --> 13:41.140
Walk me through it.

13:41.760 --> 13:45.320
Imagine a developer in 2018 is trying to fix a server issue.

13:45.680 --> 13:49.500
They write a quick, messy, totally untested hack just to see if it works.

13:49.700 --> 13:50.280
It's provisional.

13:50.560 --> 13:51.640
Just duct taping dreams.

13:51.980 --> 13:52.460
Exactly.

13:53.000 --> 13:57.160
But they post it on a forum and they don't flag its epistemic status.

13:57.640 --> 13:59.800
They don't write, hey, this is untested.

14:00.040 --> 14:00.920
Do not use in production.

14:01.200 --> 14:02.440
They just leave it unmarked.

14:02.820 --> 14:03.080
Okay.

14:03.140 --> 14:04.300
So the warning label is missing.

14:04.300 --> 14:06.540
Then someone else wanders by in 2020.

14:07.040 --> 14:07.280
Yes.

14:07.920 --> 14:11.800
A future explorer comes along, they encounter the code, and because they have the right

14:11.800 --> 14:15.660
corpus congruence, they interpret it as a viable solution.

14:16.040 --> 14:18.400
And because it lacks a warning label, they take uptake.

14:18.760 --> 14:19.120
Yes.

14:19.480 --> 14:21.120
They build it into an open source library.

14:21.620 --> 14:26.460
Now, the math in FlyAxo's model shows that if the marginal yield of this defective idea,

14:26.620 --> 14:29.900
which is the rate at which new people encounter and use that open source library,

14:30.480 --> 14:32.460
exceeds the platform's decay rate,

14:32.460 --> 14:34.740
the bad idea spreads geometrically.

14:34.940 --> 14:35.080
Oh.

14:35.680 --> 14:39.580
So because we evaluate information based on our own limited personal corpus,

14:40.200 --> 14:44.880
that original developer's duct tape hack is suddenly taken as gospel truth by thousands

14:44.880 --> 14:47.120
of people who lack the original context.

14:47.440 --> 14:48.240
It compounds.

14:49.120 --> 14:52.240
Adoption of the broken specification grows without bound,

14:52.440 --> 14:55.280
while the share of defect-free deposits actually shrinks.

14:55.600 --> 14:56.760
That is terrifying.

14:56.760 --> 14:59.720
And here is the truly terrifying part.

15:00.560 --> 15:05.200
What happens if the original author realizes in 2021 that their code was flawed,

15:05.420 --> 15:07.220
but they can't fix the problem?

15:07.500 --> 15:08.540
Wait, why couldn't they fix it?

15:09.040 --> 15:12.060
Flyxion points out that if corrections are unreachable,

15:12.760 --> 15:17.100
meaning the platform doesn't have a mechanism to push a backlink from the correction to everyone

15:17.100 --> 15:20.260
who copied the error, the spread is literally unstoppable.

15:20.260 --> 15:22.740
That is wild to think about.

15:23.480 --> 15:27.420
I mean, think about how often you just copy-paste a snippet of code from Stack Overflow

15:27.420 --> 15:32.040
or pull a template off a website without actually understanding the underlying logic.

15:32.560 --> 15:33.380
Happens all the time.

15:33.760 --> 15:38.080
We are basically letting a typo dictate the foundation of a digital skyscraper

15:38.080 --> 15:42.760
just because the typo was easier to copy and paste than doing the math from scratch.

15:42.760 --> 15:49.000
Which is exactly why Flyxion argues that explicitly marking the epistemic status of your deposit

15:49.000 --> 15:52.900
saying, this is a prototype or this has high evidential support

15:52.900 --> 15:56.780
is the only structural way to lower the uptake of a defective component.

15:57.320 --> 15:57.640
Okay.

15:58.060 --> 16:03.860
We've been talking almost exclusively about software, GitHub repositories, and digital navigation.

16:04.280 --> 16:08.420
But as I'm picturing this, the idea of passing down unverified assumptions

16:08.420 --> 16:12.460
and locking successive generations into old, flawed ways of thinking,

16:12.720 --> 16:15.640
is this really just a modern internet phenomenon?

16:16.440 --> 16:17.180
Not at all.

16:17.600 --> 16:20.920
Did we invent the berm in the 1990s?

16:21.320 --> 16:24.740
If we connect this to the bigger picture, the answer is a resounding no.

16:24.980 --> 16:26.500
The mechanism is ancient.

16:26.820 --> 16:28.940
It is quite literally as old as human culture.

16:29.520 --> 16:29.780
Really?

16:30.140 --> 16:30.340
Yeah.

16:30.820 --> 16:32.580
About two-thirds of the way through the paper,

16:33.060 --> 16:35.880
Flyxion steps entirely back from the digital realm

16:35.880 --> 16:38.640
to introduce the concept of pedagogical residue.

16:39.600 --> 16:40.600
Pedagogical residue.

16:41.340 --> 16:44.900
So we're talking about the residue left behind by teachers,

16:45.560 --> 16:48.000
universities, and educational systems.

16:48.520 --> 16:49.080
Precisely.

16:49.680 --> 16:50.980
When a teacher writes a curriculum,

16:51.380 --> 16:54.220
when a scientist designs a physical laboratory instrument,

16:54.480 --> 16:56.820
or when a professor writes a textbook,

16:57.240 --> 16:59.180
they are leaving stigmurgic berms.

16:59.760 --> 17:00.340
Oh, wow.

17:00.460 --> 17:01.780
I never thought of it like that.

17:02.080 --> 17:04.340
Think about a university syllabus for a moment.

17:04.340 --> 17:08.980
A syllabus is essentially a highly structured, shared return structure.

17:09.720 --> 17:12.120
It fixes a sequence of encounters with knowledge.

17:12.340 --> 17:12.560
Right.

17:13.080 --> 17:16.280
It provides the prerequisites, the cross-references,

17:16.640 --> 17:18.640
the paths to revisit when you get lost.

17:19.080 --> 17:21.920
So a syllabus is basically a formalized vine

17:21.920 --> 17:23.560
that the teacher hands to the student.

17:23.900 --> 17:24.200
Yes.

17:24.480 --> 17:26.520
And the textbook is the declarative residue,

17:26.940 --> 17:29.420
the blueprint that tells the student what is worth knowing

17:29.420 --> 17:31.280
and what they can safely ignore.

17:31.580 --> 17:32.380
You've got it perfectly.

17:32.380 --> 17:34.780
But consider the implication.

17:35.480 --> 17:39.620
If educational materials follow the exact same stigmurgic rules

17:39.620 --> 17:41.020
as software repositories,

17:41.240 --> 17:45.380
they are vulnerable to the exact same lock-in and viral errors.

17:45.720 --> 17:46.180
Oh, man.

17:46.760 --> 17:50.620
Educational materials brilliantly preserve obsolete conventions,

17:51.120 --> 17:54.360
institutional prejudices, and unnecessary complexity.

17:54.760 --> 17:56.460
And just like the bad code,

17:56.820 --> 17:59.280
these things are almost always unmarked.

17:59.740 --> 18:00.740
Oh, I see it.

18:00.740 --> 18:05.460
A 19th century curriculum might carry immense institutional authority,

18:05.980 --> 18:08.800
which gives it a high perceived evidential support.

18:09.180 --> 18:12.380
But it could be completely factually unsound by modern standards.

18:12.740 --> 18:13.160
Exactly.

18:13.960 --> 18:16.920
And yet, because it's easier to teach from the existing syllabus

18:16.920 --> 18:18.760
than to write a new one from scratch,

18:19.640 --> 18:22.300
successive generations of teachers just reproduce it.

18:22.300 --> 18:25.680
It's the exact same self-propagating mechanism

18:25.680 --> 18:28.800
as the defective code in example 7.12.

18:29.140 --> 18:30.040
It is identical.

18:30.640 --> 18:32.440
Flyxion argues that human intelligence

18:32.440 --> 18:35.680
isn't just something that happens inside our individual skulls.

18:35.780 --> 18:38.220
It is heavily offloaded into our environment.

18:38.580 --> 18:38.640
Right.

18:38.920 --> 18:42.480
The core human act of building cumulative culture is the same,

18:42.580 --> 18:45.260
whether we are talking about a medieval library,

18:45.480 --> 18:47.080
preserving an ancient oral tradition,

18:47.080 --> 18:51.640
or a modern software repository managing million-line code bases.

18:51.800 --> 18:55.540
So a university is basically just a giant, physical,

18:56.540 --> 18:58.480
stigmurgic berm

18:58.480 --> 19:01.380
built by centuries of academic termites

19:01.380 --> 19:04.420
carefully stacking their syllabus pellets on top of each other.

19:04.820 --> 19:06.480
As funny as that image is,

19:06.920 --> 19:07.920
mathematically, yes.

19:08.420 --> 19:10.580
The only thing that separates the medieval library

19:10.580 --> 19:13.140
from the GitHub repository is the parameters.

19:13.620 --> 19:14.280
The parameters.

19:14.280 --> 19:16.780
You mean the speed and scale of it all?

19:17.200 --> 19:17.480
Yes.

19:18.000 --> 19:19.160
The speed of persistence,

19:19.460 --> 19:20.300
the decay rate,

19:20.480 --> 19:21.740
the global accessibility,

19:22.280 --> 19:23.500
and the recomposability.

19:23.920 --> 19:24.420
How so?

19:25.080 --> 19:27.620
Well, a medieval manuscript decays slowly,

19:27.900 --> 19:30.040
but it is incredibly difficult to access

19:30.040 --> 19:33.740
and nearly impossible to quickly modify and redistribute.

19:34.340 --> 19:34.620
Right.

19:34.940 --> 19:37.880
You'd have to physically travel there and copy it by hand.

19:38.520 --> 19:39.120
Exactly.

19:39.520 --> 19:42.860
But a digital deposit can be accessed globally in milliseconds,

19:42.860 --> 19:43.900
modified, tested,

19:44.020 --> 19:45.860
and pushed back to the berm instantly.

19:46.500 --> 19:48.060
The fundamental logic of accumulation

19:48.060 --> 19:50.540
hasn't changed since we were drawing on cave walls.

19:50.680 --> 19:51.400
It's just faster now.

19:51.980 --> 19:53.620
Only the speed and granularity

19:53.620 --> 19:56.080
with which we can inherit and modify the berm

19:56.080 --> 19:56.960
has accelerated.

19:57.280 --> 19:58.540
So what does this all mean?

19:59.120 --> 20:01.000
If we zoom all the way out,

20:01.540 --> 20:02.620
Flyxion is showing us

20:02.620 --> 20:04.760
that the world we navigate every single day

20:04.760 --> 20:06.320
is built by this tight,

20:06.520 --> 20:08.680
inseparable coupling of the vine and the berm.

20:09.160 --> 20:09.780
Beautifully said.

20:09.780 --> 20:11.160
We rely on our vines,

20:11.340 --> 20:12.340
our private paths,

20:12.800 --> 20:13.400
our bookmarks,

20:13.520 --> 20:14.400
our return stacks,

20:14.540 --> 20:16.260
our video game save points

20:16.260 --> 20:17.340
to keep us grounded

20:17.340 --> 20:19.700
and make us brave enough to explore the unknown.

20:19.940 --> 20:20.020
Right.

20:20.600 --> 20:21.480
And as we explore,

20:21.900 --> 20:22.880
we drop the berm,

20:23.300 --> 20:24.140
the public residue,

20:24.420 --> 20:25.200
the working code,

20:25.320 --> 20:25.940
the blueprints,

20:26.080 --> 20:26.940
the syllabi

20:26.940 --> 20:29.940
that silently orchestrates the future for everyone else.

20:30.620 --> 20:31.640
It's wild to realize

20:31.640 --> 20:34.440
that grand, world-changing infrastructure

20:34.440 --> 20:36.300
doesn't actually need an architect.

20:36.900 --> 20:37.620
It really doesn't.

20:37.620 --> 20:39.540
It doesn't need a master plan.

20:39.720 --> 20:41.640
It just needs a population of explorers

20:41.640 --> 20:43.340
leaving a trail of breadcrumbs.

20:44.260 --> 20:45.740
This raises an important question.

20:46.900 --> 20:47.900
Flyxion has demonstrated

20:47.900 --> 20:50.160
that our future capabilities as a species

20:50.160 --> 20:51.540
depend almost entirely

20:51.540 --> 20:54.480
on the persistent discoverable residue

20:54.480 --> 20:56.360
we leave behind for the next explorer.

20:56.920 --> 20:57.120
Yeah.

20:57.260 --> 20:58.440
We have to leave the breadcrumbs.

20:58.800 --> 21:00.080
But look at the direction

21:00.080 --> 21:02.600
the modern internet is heavily trending towards.

21:02.600 --> 21:05.080
We are rapidly entering an era

21:05.080 --> 21:06.320
where digital platforms

21:06.320 --> 21:09.240
increasingly default to ephemeral content.

21:09.580 --> 21:09.840
Oh, wow.

21:10.180 --> 21:10.660
You're right.

21:11.180 --> 21:12.160
Auto-deleting messages.

21:12.980 --> 21:15.200
Stories that expire in 24 hours.

21:15.900 --> 21:17.120
Hidden algorithmic feeds

21:17.120 --> 21:19.700
that surface a piece of brilliance for one day

21:19.700 --> 21:21.240
and then bury it forever.

21:21.420 --> 21:22.540
Totally unreachable.

21:23.100 --> 21:24.500
So it never becomes part of the berm.

21:25.080 --> 21:25.640
Exactly.

21:25.960 --> 21:28.620
If human progress fundamentally requires

21:28.620 --> 21:30.600
a persistent berm to build upon,

21:30.600 --> 21:33.920
well, what happens to cumulative human culture

21:33.920 --> 21:36.240
when our deposits simply evaporate?

21:36.680 --> 21:39.220
Are we currently actively erasing

21:39.220 --> 21:41.320
the very stigmurgic berms

21:41.320 --> 21:42.600
that the next generation

21:42.600 --> 21:43.880
desperately needs to find?

21:44.200 --> 21:44.340
Wow.

21:44.980 --> 21:47.440
That is a deeply chilling thought to end on.

21:48.120 --> 21:49.680
If the residue disappears,

21:50.420 --> 21:52.120
we might be burning the blueprints

21:52.120 --> 21:53.040
before the next builder

21:53.040 --> 21:54.880
even has a chance to walk into the workshop.

21:55.880 --> 21:56.940
Thank you so much

21:56.940 --> 21:58.420
for taking this deep dive with us today.

21:58.420 --> 21:59.400
It was a pleasure.

21:59.400 --> 22:01.300
The next time you find yourself

22:01.300 --> 22:02.660
leaving 20 tabs open

22:02.660 --> 22:05.040
or hitting save on a rough draft

22:05.040 --> 22:06.820
or bookmarking a strange,

22:06.980 --> 22:08.040
wonderful corner of the internet,

22:08.400 --> 22:09.680
I want you to remember

22:09.680 --> 22:12.380
you're just leaving a breadcrumb for yourself.

22:12.780 --> 22:14.900
You might just be laying the foundation stone

22:14.900 --> 22:16.560
for whoever comes next.

